artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let H be a (real) Hilbert space and let C be a nonempty weakly closed subset of H. Let S be a left reversible semitopological semigroup; that is, S is a semigroup endowed with a Hausdorff topology for which the multiplication is separately continuous, and any two closed right ideals of S have nonempty intersection. Suppose {T_s : s ∈ S} is a representation of S as self-mappings of C such that:

(i) each T_s : C → C is norm nonexpansive, i.e. ‖T_s x − T_s y‖ ≤ ‖x − y‖ for all x, y ∈ C;
(ii) the map (s, x) ↦ T_s x from S × C to C is jointly continuous when C carries the weak topology of H;
(iii) there exists c ∈ C such that the orbit {T_s c : s ∈ S} is bounded in norm.

Question: Must there exist a common fixed point of S in C, i.e. a point x ∈ C with T_s x = x for every s ∈ S?

(It is known that the answer is affirmative when S is separable. The problem asks whether the separability hypothesis can be removed.)

